-- | Genesis propose/accept (phase 4 of the participant-split redesign, plan §5.1 at
-- .claude/tasks/e2e-separate-participants.md, reconciled with the CIP-56/namespace-retirement
-- rework's real genesis per the integration plan's §8.1). 'Playground.Init:initPlayground' used
-- to create 'Wormhole.Core.State:CoreState' with a single `submit (actAs operator <> actAs
-- guardianGovernance)` -- a co-signed create that needs one script user with actAs rights on
-- BOTH parties. That only worked because operator and guardianGovernance were hosted on the
-- same (only) participant. Now that guardianGovernance has its own participant (the topology
-- split, plan's §2), no single `dpm script` invocation can submit as both -- genesis is a
-- propose (on operator's participant) / accept (on guardianGovernance's participant) pair
-- instead. 'Playground.Init' is split accordingly into `proposeGenesis`/`acceptGenesis`.
--
-- 'AcceptGenesis' bootstraps BOTH roots in the one ceremony: the core 'CoreState' AND the NTT
-- root 'Wormhole.Ntt.Governance:NttGovernance' -- both need operator+guardianGovernance
-- co-authorization, and per 'NttGovernance''s own header this is "the only time gg actively
-- signs for NTT". Folding it into this same accept choice (rather than a separate
-- single-participant co-signed create, which 'Playground.Init' used pre-split) means gg signs
-- ONCE for both roots, exactly as the pre-split single-shot genesis did.
module Playground.Genesis where

import DA.Set qualified as Set
import Splice.Api.Token.HoldingV1 (InstrumentId)
import Wormhole.Core.Bytes (Bytes20, Bytes32)
import Wormhole.Core.State (CoreState)
import Wormhole.Core.Setup (mkInitialCoreState)
import Wormhole.Ntt.Governance (NttGovernance (..))

-- | The operator's genesis offer: every argument 'Wormhole.Core.Setup:mkInitialCoreState'
-- needs, held on a contract only the operator signs -- guardianGovernance sees it as an
-- ordinary stakeholder (no explicit disclosure needed, unlike the cross-participant
-- disclosure mechanism in Playground.Disclose) and accepts from its OWN participant.
template CoreStateBootstrapProposal
  with
    operator           : Party
    guardianGovernance : Party
    governanceChainId  : Int
    governanceContract : Bytes32
    initialGuardians   : [Bytes20]
    guardianObserver   : Party
    feeInstrument      : InstrumentId
    feeRecipient       : Party
  where
    signatory operator
    observer guardianGovernance

    -- Creating 'CoreState'/'NttGovernance' from inside this choice's body carries BOTH this
    -- proposal's signatory (operator, via the proposal contract being consumed) AND the
    -- exercising controller's (guardianGovernance) authority, satisfying CoreState's `signatory
    -- operator, guardianGovernance` and NttGovernance's `signatory guardianGovernance,
    -- operator` invariants without any single script needing actAs rights on both parties at
    -- once -- exactly the propose/accept split genesis needs post-split.
    choice AcceptGenesis : (ContractId CoreState, ContractId NttGovernance)
      controller guardianGovernance
      do
        csCid <- create (mkInitialCoreState operator guardianGovernance governanceChainId
                  governanceContract initialGuardians guardianObserver feeInstrument
                  feeRecipient)
        govCid <- create NttGovernance with operator, guardianGovernance, nextId = 0, consumedGovernance = Set.empty
        pure (csCid, govCid)
